st so i thought why  can't we just work together  our collaboration has lasted for almost  10 years you know we started from  several  international meetings we then began to  exchange  our researcher so we talk reasonably  often but at the moment we talk  mainly through hong kong so sometimes  hong jenny and i will be in a meeting  and we'll be discussing  you know something to do with the  experiment and i will say  you know off the top of my head oh hong  could you ask  our duncan colleagues about this she  will almost instantaneously get onto her  phone get onto wechat  and within often half an hour we have an  answer to the question  we're looking at this very surface of  the earth and cube  and we can see the bits that have been  um subjected to the harshest conditions  have become  far more rough whereas at the start it  was very very smooth  is the culture of doing science  different between the uk  i believe there are quite significant  differences yes  some of those i think are generational  so i think if if you were a scientist in  the  starting off in the 70s and 80s in china  then their their systems were quite  isolated from the west and i think they  had very specific  viewpoints i think there are some  differences  really especially the way of thinking  scientific problems i felt  the science in the uk is more like  philosophy  chinese scientists are more focused on  solving  practical problems that's quite unusual  in britain the science is often quite a  long  way away from the the practicalities  another  huge difference between two teams is  is the way of expression you know  western people always dare to express  their opinions while our culture tear us  we should thinking more before we talk  the  really obvious cultural difference that  sticks in my mind is just the difference  between  work and home i think they found it  rather strange  when they came as individuals to spend  time here for a year  and they think i think they felt that i  was rather cold because i  would come to meetings with people but i  wouldn't invite them round to my  house and you know i like to have some  down time  so after dinner was like right so you  put your meat in to work out what's  happening tomorrow  yeah that that's why you say why i said  that  to jenny probably i i feel a little bit  sorry because i know for jenny  who's uh growing up here uh probably  wouldn't  like uh do that kind of things uh  after outside the working hours but  that is very normal in china are there  any more examples of things that you  have to tell  jenny or heather or you know english  researchers um  when they told me to sit in the van  because it was too much work i'm telling  you not to get offended by it  yeah yeah yeah and i mean in china for  carrying out those field work mostly  just a man  she already noticed when jenny was there  those chinese guys they thought oh she's  a student  firstly secondly she's  a girl and you know under this  very hot weather it's too too tough for  for a lady they just try to  for example like treat her like a sister  try to protect  her but without realizing properly that  could be a problem in a western country  this is an environmental cabinet and  it's used  to simulate environmental conditions so  we're simulating  what happens in winter so when it's  really cold during the night and then  warms up during the day  chinese people are very hospitality  uh the each time we we don't need a  pay like a food accommodation  i think after so many years has already  changed the something so when they  when chinese friends i mean chinese  colleague  uh come here uh each time  has tried to organize a nice  meal for them just to show  her you know appreciation  so i think somehow by the collaboration  i mean people just uh  learn from each other so my english  is not very good so you have to  say your english is not very good yeah  not not  good enough  [Laughter]  many challenges and difficulties occur  in such a  transnational teamwork such as  difference  in language time lagger  cultural highway there is difference  we can overcome compared with the  benefits  from our collaboration one of the areas  that we found to be quite challenging  is deciding whose name gets on to the  paper that  comes out as a result of the experiments  we're doing  and what order the names are in and what  we've discovered is that chinese  practice is quite different  to to to british practice and i think  it's a measure of the success of our  collaboration that we've been able to  have some quite  uncomfortable conversations and come up  with something that  works at the international agreed level  of  kind of research ethics although we  sometimes have different  opinions we can research  quickly because we have the same goal  which is to protect the cultural  heritages  which belong to all of mankind  the big sort of political goings-on how  do you deal with the fact that they're  there  and you do talk about them at all um  that's a really good question i mean  politics is always just  very narrowly under the surface my  experience is that it's really hard to  talk to chinese colleagues in any  meaningful way about these things  even when they're in britain they're  quite reticent  about being being open and it's quite  interesting for them to be sat in our  lab group when we're having some  political argument and you can tell they  feel quite  uncomfortable and i think when we go to  china we also feel uncomfortable  because you know we see all the cameras  taking pictures of  every vehicle as you go along motorways  and i think each one of us as  individuals  has to navigate that we are  comparing p sorry i said p we  again because we are comparing  sorry i think there's a real  challenge in trying to actually get to  know and understand  each other and the way that we've really  i  think got round that challenge is  through spending as much time together  as we  as we can and also  luckily perhaps sharing a good sense of  humor  and that would be my tip for any  collaboration actually  is that if you can laugh together you  can probably  start to really understand each other  well the basic  prerequisites is trust when you  have doubts you can ask questions  but do not rush to thank the other  team before you fully understand  if i was going to advise colleagues who  are wanting to start out on these  collaborations i would say be humble  i think one of the dangers that i've  learned and this is through  collaborating with people anywhere in  the world  is do not spout what you think  is something really sensible in the  first five minutes  always listening to people trying to  be humble and say i don't really know  what what do you think  is a much better way of of of getting a  shared  understanding of things  perhaps the best benefit that i've had  from this collaboration  is when i go to china and i have to give  a lecture and it has to be translated  slide by slide into chinese and it takes  forever and it's really stressful and at  the end  i get so many really interesting  questions  and i've just been exposed to a lot of  different ideas and viewpoints  you know heza is very clever and so kind  it's very relaxed stay with her and her  teams  she's always about able to put  herself in your shoes she's my really  friend  during our corporation i've absolutely  loved collaborating with them i mean  they've  they've shown me sight that i would  never have seen otherwise  they've just given me experience to work  with different people in  in different ways i've i've really  enjoyed it  for me i think firstly even i'm working  here  i can also contribute to  those historical sites  in china so this is a kind of you know  the feeling feel very happy  about it

